.TH SH-PDE 1
.SH NAME
pde \- shell script interface to pde(2)'s problem-specification language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load pde

.B ${pde
.B new
.B }
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B mesh
.I NXxNY
.RB [ domain
.IR WxH ]
.RB [ bc
.IR clamp|periodic|zero ]
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B equation diffuse diffusivity
.I D
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B equation wave speed
.I S
.B damping
.I Dm
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B equation gray da
.I Da
.B db
.I Db
.B feed
.I F
.B kill
.I K
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B "solver explicit"
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B solver gmres|cg
.RB [ tolerance
.IR T ]
.RB [ restart
.IR R ]
.RB [ maxiter
.IR N ]
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B time step
.I DT
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B commit
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B step
.I dt
.br
.B ${pde
.I id
.B run
.I until
.B }
.br
.B ${pde
.I id
.B get
.I x y
.B }
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B set
.I x y value
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B splat
.I cx cy radius value
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B clear
.I value
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B setb
.I x y value
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B splatb
.I cx cy radius value
.br
.B pde
.I id
.B clearb
.I value
.br
.B ${pde
.I id
.B getb
.I x y
.B }
.br
.B ${pde
.I id
.B nx|ny|dx|dy
.B }
.br
.B pde del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pde
is a loadable module for
.IR sh (1)
giving shell scripts direct access to
.IR pde (2)'s
own little language for specifying an entire problem \(em mesh,
equation, and solver together \(em declaratively, the way a scientist
would write it, instead of a compiled Limbo program.
.PP
.IR Pde (2)'s
own
.B newproblem
takes a whole spec as one string in a single call; this module adds
the incremental, script-friendly build-up around it \(em
.BR mesh / equation / solver / time
accumulate one line of the spec each,
.B commit
then calls
.IR pde (2)'s
.B newproblem
on the accumulated lines \(em without changing
.IR pde (2)
itself in any way: every verb below except the handle bookkeeping is
exactly
.IR pde (2)'s
own vocabulary, documented there in full.
.PP
.B ${pde new}
allocates a handle (a small integer) for a problem still being built,
and yields it.
.BR Mesh ,
.BR equation ,
.BR solver ,
and
.B time
each append one line of spec text to that handle, exactly as
.IR pde (2)
defines them; using any of them after
.B commit
raises an error.
.B Commit
parses the accumulated spec via
.IR pde (2)'s
.B newproblem
and turns the handle into a ready problem; using
.BR step ,
.BR run ,
.BR get ,
.BR set ,
.BR splat ,
.BR clear ,
or the
.B nx
/
.B ny
/
.B dx
/
.B dy
introspection verbs before committing raises an error.
.PP
.B Step
advances the problem by an explicit
.I dt
(for an interactive caller driving its own loop).
.B Run
is the batch driver: it repeats
.B step
using the handle's own
.B "time step"
line until at least
.I until
total time has elapsed, and yields the number of steps taken \(em what
a verification or UQ study run from a shell script calls instead of
.BR step .
Both raise an error (e.g. a solver that failed to converge) rather than
leaving the field holding a garbage state.
.PP
.BR Get ,
.BR set ,
.BR splat ,
and
.B clear
read or write the underlying field directly, the same as
.IR pde (2)'s
own functions of the same name.
.PP
.BR Getb ,
.BR setb ,
.BR splatb ,
and
.B clearb
do the same for the second field
.RB ( fieldb )
that only exists for
.BR "equation gray" ;
using any of them on a handle whose equation isn't
.B gray
raises an error.
.B Del
frees one or more handles.
.PP
A complete example \(em an implicit diffusion solve, stepped to
completion, matching the same problem
.IR pde (2)'s
own SYNOPSIS shows in Limbo:
.EX
	load pde
	id := ${pde new}
	pde $id mesh 64x64 domain 1x1 bc clamp
	pde $id equation diffuse diffusivity 0.05
	pde $id solver gmres tolerance 1e-8 restart 30
	pde $id time step 0.01
	pde $id commit
	pde $id clear 0.0
	pde $id set 32 32 1.0
	n := ${pde $id run 10.0}
	echo centre ${pde $id get 32 32}
	pde del $id
.EE
